{%- doc -%}
  How it is used, as a numbered rail, from custom.steps.

  A rule is drawn between the numbers so the block reads as an order of
  operations rather than as a pile of tips. The last step does not draw it, so
  the sequence visibly ends.

  A demonstration film can sit above the rail. For this product it is worth
  more than the eight steps under it: the single hardest thing to explain
  about Bloom is that the animal goes through the mesh rather than standing on
  it, and eight seconds of footage settles that without a word. The steps stay
  because they carry what film cannot: the order, the acclimatisation, and
  when to stop.

  It plays muted and looping like an animation, with controls, so it explains
  itself to someone who never touches it and can still be paused and scrubbed
  by someone who wants to look closely. Autoplay is a setting rather than a
  given, because a looping film is not what everyone wants on a page.
{%- enddoc -%}

{%- liquid
  assign steps = product.metafields.custom.steps.value
-%}

{%- if steps.size > 0 or section.settings.demo != blank -%}
  <section class="wf-band wf-band--bark">
    <div class="pl-wrap">
      <div class="wf-band__head">
        <h2 class="wf-h2">{{ section.settings.heading }}</h2>
        {%- if section.settings.text != blank -%}
          <p class="wf-sub">{{ section.settings.text }}</p>
        {%- endif -%}
      </div>

      {%- if section.settings.demo != blank -%}
        <div class="wf-demo">
          {{
            section.settings.demo
            | video_tag:
              image_size: '1200x',
              controls: true,
              muted: true,
              loop: section.settings.autoplay,
              autoplay: section.settings.autoplay,
              playsinline: true,
              preload: 'metadata',
              class: 'wf-demo__v'
          }}
          {%- if section.settings.demo_caption != blank -%}
            <p class="wf-demo__c">{{ section.settings.demo_caption }}</p>
          {%- endif -%}
        </div>
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- if steps.size > 0 -%}
      <ol class="wf-steps">
        {%- for item in steps -%}
          <li class="wf-step">
            <div class="wf-step__n" aria-hidden="true">{{ forloop.index }}</div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="wf-step__t">{{ item.title }}</h3>
              <p class="wf-step__x">{{ item.text }}</p>
            </div>
          </li>
        {%- endfor -%}
      </ol>
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>
  </section>

  {%- if section.settings.autoplay -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      A film that plays itself is motion nobody asked for, and CSS cannot stop
      it: prefers-reduced-motion can turn off an animation or a transition,
      but autoplay on a video element is not either of those. So the check is
      made here and the film is paused and rewound. The controls are already
      on it, so anyone who wants it still has it, which is the whole point of
      the preference rather than hiding the thing.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <script>
      (function () {
        if (!window.matchMedia) return;
        if (!window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches) return;
        document.querySelectorAll('.wf-demo video').forEach(function (v) {
          v.autoplay = false;
          v.loop = false;
          v.pause();
          v.currentTime = 0;
        });
      })();
    </script>
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endif -%}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "מוצר · איך משתמשים",
  "tag": "section",
  "settings": [
    { "type": "text", "id": "heading", "label": "כותרת", "default": "איך משתמשים בזה" },
    { "type": "textarea", "id": "text", "label": "טקסט מתחת לכותרת" },
    { "type": "header", "content": "סרטון הדגמה" },
    { "type": "video", "id": "demo", "label": "סרטון (מהקבצים של החנות)" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "demo_caption", "label": "כיתוב מתחת לסרטון" },
    { "type": "checkbox", "id": "autoplay", "label": "להפעיל אוטומטית בלולאה, בלי קול", "default": true }
  ],
  "presets": [{ "name": "מוצר · איך משתמשים" }]
}
{% endschema %}
